<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ghilzai</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ghilzai Afghans</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ghilzai are a large Pashtun tribal confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Persecuted for their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> faith, the Ghilzai of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> rebelled against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirwais-hotak">Mir Wais</a> and in 1722 captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a>, expelled them from Isfahan in 1729.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ghilzai.jpg" alt="Ghilzai" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Burke (1843-1900) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sirdar_Habibullah_Gilzai_and_other_Khans_in_1879-80.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Ghilji, also spelled Ghilzai, Khilji and Khalji, are one of the largest Pashtun tribes. Their traditional homeland is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> and Qalati Ghilji in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, and they make up around 20–25% of Afghanistan's population. According to the historian C. E. Bosworth the name derives from that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalaj">Khalaj</a>, a people usually referred to as Turks who, he thinks, formed the core of the tribe. A popular folk etymology instead derives it from Gharzay, meaning born of the mountain. Another account holds that the Ghilji descend from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>. The 17th-century courtier Nimat Allah al-Harawi gave a mythical genealogy tracing them to Shah Hussain Ghori.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Geographers of the 9th and 10th centuries, Ibn Khordadbeh and Istakhri, wrote that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalaj">Khalaj</a> had crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> and settled around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>; the Hudud al-'Alam described them as sheep-grazing nomads. Al-Utbi wrote that when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabuktigin">Sabuktigin</a> defeated the Hindu Shahi ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapala">Jayapala</a> in 988, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtuns</a> and Khalaj between Laghman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> agreed to serve him, and that Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> recruited them in large numbers. In 1221 Khalaj and other tribesmen in the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalal-al-din-mangburni">Jalal al-Din Mangburni</a> helped defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> at the Battle of Parwan, then left over the booty.</p><p>In April 1709 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirwais-hotak">Mirwais Hotak</a>, of the Hotak tribe of the Ghilji, led a revolution against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> and founded the Hotak dynasty at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>, declaring southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> independent. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-hotak">Mahmud Hotak</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in 1722, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> remained the dynasty's capital for six years. The dynasty ended in 1738, when Nader Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afshar-tribe">Afshar</a> defeated its last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussain-hotak">Hussain Hotak</a>, at the Battle of Kandahar. Azad Khan Afghan, of the Andar tribe, rose to power in western Iran between 1752 and 1757.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-afghan-war">First Anglo-Afghan War</a> (1839–1842), a Ghilji force attacked the British Indian garrison retreating from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> on 6 January 1842. On 12 January the last survivors were killed or captured near Gandamak, and only the surgeon William Brydon reached Jalalabad, on 13 January. After the Ghilji rebelled in 1886, Emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdur-rahman-khan">Abdur Rahman Khan</a> forced many of them to migrate to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Ghilji of the central region speak Central Pashto, a dialect transitional between the southern and northern varieties. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> they live chiefly between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durand-line">Durand Line</a>, a line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, and Jalalabad. The modern nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Kochi</a> are predominantly Ghilji, and before the 1947 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">partition of India</a> some Ghilji wintered there as nomadic merchants, carrying goods by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> caravan to sell or barter in Afghanistan. Their subtribes include the Ahmadzai, Hotak, Andar, Kharoti, Tokhi and Sulaimankhel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ghilji” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He defeated the <b>Ghilzai Afghans</b> at Damghan and Murche-Khort and expelled them from Isfahan in 1729, freeing the Safavid capital after seven years of occupation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1709 the <b>Ghilzai Afghans</b> of Kandahar, Sunnis resentful of Safavid rule, rose under Mir Wais Hotak.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1709 Mirwais Hotak, a chief of the <b>Ghilji Pashtuns</b>, led a revolt in Kandahar against the Safavid governor Gurgin Khan, killed him and made Kandahar independent.</blockquote><a href="/state/hotak-dynasty">Hotak dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
